Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Mild Cutaneous
Complex Fatigue Entities
SLE with predominantly skin/joint manifestations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-048
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe generalized joint pain, intense facial rash, and extreme physical exhaustion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 24-hour escalation of severe, immobilizing arthralgia, a burning facial rash, and profound systemic fatigue preventing the patient from standing or performing basic self-care. Immediate physical assessment was required to rule out acute rheumatological joint destruction or systemic infection, and to provide potent systemic anti-inflammatories before morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every joint in my body is throbbing, my face is burning up, and I am too exhausted to even get out of bed.
History of Present Illness: Patient with recently diagnosed SLE presents with a severe acute exacerbation triggered by brief sun exposure yesterday. Symptoms include an intensely burning malar rash across the cheeks and nasal bridge, marked photosensitivity, and severe symmetrical arthralgia affecting the wrists, knees, and small joints of the hands. The patient reports a pervasive fatigue that feels like a total systemic shut-down.
Functional Status: Bedbound tonight due to extreme fatigue and severe joint pain upon movement.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to access the kitchen or prepare food safely due to hand pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Fatigued, guarding all joints, keeping the room completely dark. Low-grade fever present.
Physical Exam: Facial exam reveals a classic, bright red, slightly raised erythematous malar rash sparing the nasolabial folds. Joint exam shows diffuse, boggy, tender swelling (synovitis) symmetrically across bilateral wrists and MCP joints, with moderate effusions in both knees. No focal, unilateral hot joint. Chest clear; normal heart sounds with no friction rub. Clinical picture maps perfectly to an acute flare of SLE with predominantly skin/joint manifestations.
Environmental Observations: All blinds drawn to eliminate UV light exposure.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently on Hydroxychloroquine. Took Paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Mild Cutaneous - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, polyarticular involvement alongside a distinct facial rash, rather than a single, severely inflamed, hot joint.

Cellulitis (Facial) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical malar distribution clearly sparing the nasolabial folds, and absence of high-grade spiking fever.

Dermatomyositis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of profound proximal muscle weakness and lack of classic heliotrope rash over the eyelids, though overlap syndromes exist.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of severe polyarticular immobility and an intensely burning facial rash mandated an urgent home visit to clinically exclude dangerous differential diagnoses such as septic arthritis or facial cellulitis. After confirming an acute, severe autoimmune flare of their established SLE, urgent intervention with systemic corticosteroids was necessary. The patient was in a state of severe pain and functional collapse; withholding treatment until the next in-hours period would have likely precipitated a fall or an unnecessary emergency department transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short, rapid-tapering course of oral Prednisolone (starting at 20mg daily for 3 days) to forcefully suppress the acute systemic autoimmune flare. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Naltrexone, Vitamin D, and Omega-3. Advised strict avoidance of all UV light.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your steroid medication strictly in the morning with a full meal, you will maximize its absorption while matching your body's natural hormone cycle, directly reducing stomach upset and rewarding yourself with improved daytime joint mobility and less sleep disruption.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: None required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP and Rheumatologist review in 5 days to assess flare resolution and escalate systemic immunomodulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops sharp, stabbing chest pain when taking a deep breath (pleuritis), sudden shortness of breath, or if a single joint becomes disproportionately swollen and hot.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While this flare is predominantly affecting the skin and joints, SLE is a systemic disease requiring regular monitoring of kidney and cardiovascular function.
Clinical Course: The oral steroids will rapidly diminish the joint swelling and reduce the intense burning of the facial rash within 24-48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you develop sharp chest pain when you breathe in, or if you find yourself suddenly struggling to breathe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system was triggered by the sun exposure to violently attack your skin and joint linings; the prescribed steroid acts as a powerful, immediate off-switch for this widespread inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Strictly apply a high-SPF sunscreen every single morning, even if remaining indoors; completely blocking all UV exposure prevents the immune system from receiving further trigger signals, positively reinforcing the rapid healing of your skin and joints.

